/* Daily lesson content based on the approved BOW and supplied ILAW lesson plans. */
window.week2to10Content = {"2":{"title":"HTML Introduction: Structure, Elements, Attributes, and HTML Editors","competencies":"Distinguish HTML structure, elements, and attributes.\nUtilize an HTML editor in creating HTML documents.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – Understanding HTML and the HTML Document Structure","discussion":"Introduce HTML as a markup language used to structure webpage content. Discuss the purpose of <!DOCTYPE html>, html, head, title, and body. Show a simple HTML document and identify opening and closing tags.","activities":"Show a sample webpage and ask: What parts of this page might be represented by code? Learners identify the visible title, heading, and paragraph. Explain the document skeleton and tag functions. Use a short think-pair-share to check understanding.","task":"Learners label the parts of a sample HTML document and explain the function of each main tag.","check":"Exit ticket: identify the purpose of head, title, and body. Ask learners to write the basic order of the document structure.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – HTML Elements, Attributes, and HTML Editors","discussion":"Explain elements, tags, opening and closing tags, and attributes such as href, src, and title. Introduce the available text/HTML editor and browser preview.","activities":"Demonstrate creating a new file, typing a simple HTML skeleton, and saving it with an .html extension. Explain the difference between saving a text file and an HTML file. Discuss safe file naming and folder organization.","task":"Pairs identify elements and attributes in sample code. Learners practice opening the editor and locating Save As and browser commands where devices are available.","check":"Short identification exercise: classify examples as tag/element/attribute. Check that learners can state why the .html extension matters.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – Build the HTML Skeleton and Fix the Broken Web Page","discussion":"Reinforce correct tag order and syntax. Focus on recognizing missing angle brackets, missing closing tags, misplaced tags, and incorrect nesting.","activities":"Model how to arrange mixed tags into a valid skeleton. Display a deliberately incorrect code sample and think aloud while locating errors. Guide learners to explain why each correction is needed.","task":"Small groups arrange mixed tag cards into a document skeleton, then annotate and correct a broken HTML sample. Learners who have device access may type and preview the corrected code.","check":"Collect corrected code or tag sequence. Ask learners to explain at least two corrections.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Practice: My First HTML Web Page (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Learners create a basic HTML document using an editor, save it, and open it in a browser.","activities":"Review the task and demonstrate only the essential steps: create file, type structure, add title/heading/paragraph, save as myfirstpage.html, and preview in browser. Divide learners into practical rotation groups. Rotation A receives priority computer time; learners waiting complete the paper preparation task.","task":"Each learner creates or begins an individual webpage. Learners waiting sketch the expected page, write the filename, arrange the code skeleton, and identify the tags they will use. Teacher checks progress and provides troubleshooting.","check":"Use the practical checklist: correct structure, meaningful title, heading and paragraph, correct .html filename, successful browser preview. Record who has completed individual computer practice.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Practice: Edit, Improve, and Demonstrate (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Learners complete the individual HTML page and demonstrate the edit-save-refresh cycle.","activities":"Briefly review common errors from Day 4. Demonstrate editing text, saving again, and refreshing the browser. Prioritize learners who still need individual computer time; other learners improve their pages or peer-check using the checklist.","task":"All learners must receive an individual turn at the computer across Days 4–5. Learners who already completed the core task make one teacher-approved improvement and explain the change. Learners still waiting complete the paper preparation sheet before their practical turn.","check":"Individual practical check and short reflection: What did you change? What happens when you save but do not refresh the browser? Record completion and support needs.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 2 – Learner Notes\nHTML Structure, Elements, Attributes, and HTML Editor\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nHTML and document structure\nHTML (HyperText Markup Language) organizes webpage content using elements. A basic document begins with <!DOCTYPE html>, followed by <html>. The <head> contains page information such as <title>; the <body> contains visible page content.\nElements and tags\nAn element commonly includes an opening tag, content, and closing tag, such as <p>Welcome.</p>. Some elements, such as <img>, are void elements and do not wrap text content.\nAttributes\nAttributes add information inside an opening tag. In <img src='photo.jpg' alt='School garden'>, src identifies the image file and alt gives a text alternative. Attribute values are usually quoted.\nHTML editors and browsers\nAn HTML editor is used to write and save code. Save a document with the .html extension, open it in a browser, and refresh the browser after saving edits.\nOrganizing files safely\nKeep related files in one project folder, use clear file names, check extensions, and use media only when you have permission.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 2 – Activities\nHTML Structure, Elements, Attributes, and HTML Editor\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: Build the HTML Skeleton\nArrange the basic tags in the correct order: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html>, <head>, <title>, </title>, </head>, <body>, </body>, </html>. Explain the function of each part.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Fix the Broken Web Page\nCorrect this sample: <html><head><title>My Page</head><body><h1>Welcome<h1><p>This is my page</body></html>. Rewrite it and explain at least three corrections.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 3: My First Web Page\nCreate myfirstpage.html with a title, one main heading, one paragraph, and one subheading. Save it and open it in a browser.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 4: Edit and Improve the Web Page\nOpen your saved page, change its title, add a paragraph, correct missing closing tags, save, and refresh the browser.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 2 – Quiz\nHTML Structure, Elements, Attributes, and HTML Editor\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. Which language structures webpage content?\nA. SQL\nB. HTML\nC. CSS\nD. JavaScript\n2. Which declaration identifies a modern HTML document?\nA. <start>\nB. <doctype>\nC. <!DOCTYPE html>\nD. <html5>\n3. Which element contains visible webpage content?\nA. <head>\nB. <title>\nC. <meta>\nD. <body>\n4. Which element contains page information such as its title?\nA. <head>\nB. <body>\nC. <footer>\nD. <main>\n5. Where are HTML attributes normally written?\nA. Outside the document\nB. Inside the opening tag\nC. After the closing tag\nD. In visible paragraph text\n6. In an image element, what does src identify?\nA. The page title\nB. The browser name\nC. The image file location or name\nD. The image's text alternative\n7. What should you do after saving an HTML edit to see it in the browser?\nA. Delete the file\nB. Change the computer date\nC. Rename the browser\nD. Refresh the browser page\n8. What is the usual file extension for an HTML document?\nA. .html\nB. .css\nC. .jpg\nD. .mp3\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."},"3":{"title":"HTML Headings, Paragraphs, Styles, and Formatting","competencies":"Apply heading, paragraph, styles, and formatting elements in an HTML document.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – HTML Headings and Paragraphs","discussion":"Discuss heading levels and paragraph elements and how they organize webpage information.","activities":"Compare a page with no structure and a page with clear headings and paragraphs. Demonstrate h1–h6 and p. Emphasize meaningful heading order rather than choosing a heading only for its size.","task":"Learners arrange sample content under suitable headings and identify where paragraphs should be placed.","check":"Quick check: select appropriate heading levels for a given page outline.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – Text Formatting Elements","discussion":"Discuss text formatting elements included in the lesson, such as bold/strong, italic/em, and underline where appropriate. Explain that formatting should support meaning and readability.","activities":"Demonstrate formatting short phrases in HTML. Show the browser result and compare source code with the displayed text.","task":"Learners mark phrases in a sample paragraph that should be emphasized and write the corresponding HTML tags.","check":"Check correct opening/closing tags and appropriate use of formatting.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – Styles and Design My Info Page Planning","discussion":"Discuss the basic style options covered in the lesson, including text color, size, or alignment. Guide learners in planning an informational webpage.","activities":"Demonstrate a simple information page and show how style choices affect readability. Model planning a title, two sections, and supporting paragraphs.","task":"Learners prepare a paper wireframe for Design My Info Page and identify the headings, paragraphs, and formatting they will apply.","check":"Review wireframes and code plans before practical work.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Activity: Design My Info Page (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Create a one-page informational webpage with headings, paragraphs, and formatted text.","activities":"Demonstrate the task requirements and a short sample. Assign practical rotation groups and explain that every learner must personally type and test the page.","task":"Learners create the page using the editor. Waiting learners write the content plan, sketch the layout, and annotate the tags and formatting to be used.","check":"Check main heading, at least two subheadings, paragraphs, and three appropriate formatting examples.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Activity: Fix and Style the Web Page (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Edit an existing webpage by correcting headings, organizing paragraphs, and applying suitable formatting.","activities":"Model how to open an existing file, identify issues, make changes, save, and refresh. Prioritize learners who have not yet completed individual practical work.","task":"Learners complete their page or improve a provided page. Every learner demonstrates at least one edit on the computer; waiting learners use the printed error-correction task before their turn.","check":"Practical checklist and reflection: Which formatting choice improved readability? What error did you correct?\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 3 – Learner Notes\nHTML Headings, Paragraphs, Styles, and Formatting\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nHeadings\nHTML provides six heading levels, <h1> through <h6>. Use headings to organize content by importance and section. Use a logical order rather than choosing a heading only for its size.\nParagraphs\nThe <p> element marks a paragraph. Use separate paragraph elements for separate ideas so content is easier to read.\nText formatting\n<strong> marks strong importance and <em> marks emphasis. <b> and <i> can apply bold or italic presentation without necessarily expressing the same semantic meaning.\nInline styles\nThe style attribute applies CSS declarations directly to an element. Example: <p style='color: blue;'>Blue text</p>. A declaration has a property and value separated by a colon; multiple declarations are separated by semicolons.\nReadability and accessibility\nUse clear headings, focused paragraphs, readable font sizes, and sufficient color contrast. Do not rely on color alone to communicate important meaning.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 3 – Activities\nHTML Headings, Paragraphs, Styles, and Formatting\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: Design My Info Page\nCreate a one-page information page about a hobby or interest. Include one <h1>, at least two <h2> headings, three paragraphs, and two formatting elements such as <strong> and <em>.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Fix and Style the Web Page\nCorrect heading order, separate long text into paragraphs, and apply suitable text formatting to an existing sample page.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 3 – Quiz\nHTML Headings, Paragraphs, Styles, and Formatting\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. Which element represents the highest heading level?\nA. <header>\nB. <h1>\nC. <h6>\nD. <head>\n2. Which element marks a paragraph?\nA. <text>\nB. <pg>\nC. <p>\nD. <para>\n3. Which element indicates strong importance?\nA. <small>\nB. <sub>\nC. <mark>\nD. <strong>\n4. Which element commonly marks emphasized text?\nA. <em>\nB. <emphasis-text>\nC. <italicize>\nD. <u>\n5. Where can inline CSS be written?\nA. After the closing html tag\nB. In an element's style attribute\nC. In the src attribute\nD. Only in the title text\n6. In color: blue;, what is blue?\nA. The HTML element\nB. The file extension\nC. The property value\nD. The selector\n7. Why use headings in logical order?\nA. To make every heading the same size\nB. To replace paragraphs\nC. To stop images loading\nD. To organize sections and support navigation\n8. Which practice improves readability?\nA. Use clear headings, focused paragraphs, and sufficient contrast\nB. Use tiny text everywhere\nC. Use color as the only signal\nD. Put all content in one paragraph\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."},"4":{"title":"HTML Multimedia, Lists, and Tables","competencies":"Embed multimedia elements in a webpage.\nPerform adding list in a webpage.\nCreate a table in a webpage.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – Embedding Images and Multimedia","discussion":"Discuss image, audio, and video elements and the importance of correct file paths and alternative text.","activities":"Demonstrate how to place a teacher-approved media file in the project folder and reference it correctly. Explain that the file must exist at the specified location. Discuss responsible use of media and copyright.","task":"Learners inspect sample code and identify the media element, source path, and alt text. If devices are available, practice locating a project folder.","check":"Exit ticket: explain why a media file may not appear and how alt text helps.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – Creating Ordered and Unordered Lists","discussion":"Discuss the purpose and syntax of ordered and unordered lists and list items.","activities":"Demonstrate ul, ol, and li with examples. Show how to organize steps versus a collection of items.","task":"Learners convert a short paragraph into an ordered or unordered list and correct a sample list with errors.","check":"Check list type selection, nesting, and closing tags.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – Creating HTML Tables","discussion":"Discuss table, row, header cell, and data cell elements. Explain when a table is useful for presenting related information.","activities":"Demonstrate a simple table such as a class task schedule or sample price list. Model how to identify rows and columns and review the browser output.","task":"Learners design a small table on paper, label the rows and columns, and complete a code-ordering exercise.","check":"Check that learners can distinguish table row, header cell, and data cell.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Activity: Add Media, Lists, and a Table (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Build a webpage containing lists, a table, and suitable multimedia when available.","activities":"Demonstrate the expected output and file organization. Provide approved media files in a shared class folder or provide a no-media alternative if unavailable. Begin practical rotation.","task":"Learners create the lists and table and add the provided image/audio/video if available. Waiting learners sketch the page, design the table, and write the list content and file paths.","check":"Check both list types, table structure, and media display when media is supplied.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Activity: Multimedia Fix and Improve (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Correct missing or broken multimedia references and improve lists and tables.","activities":"Model troubleshooting: check filename, extension, folder, path, and tag syntax. Prioritize learners who still need individual computer time.","task":"Learners test and correct their webpage. Each learner demonstrates at least one correction or improvement on the computer. Waiting learners complete the printed debugging task before their turn.","check":"Practical checklist: lists display, table is organized, media path works where provided, and page opens in browser.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 4 – Learner Notes\nHTML Multimedia, Lists, and Tables\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nImages\nThe <img> element displays an image. The src attribute identifies the file and alt provides a text description for users who cannot see the image or when it fails to load.\nAudio and video\nThe <audio> and <video> elements embed media. The controls attribute lets users play, pause, and adjust media. Check that the file path and format are correct.\nFile paths\nA relative path points to a file in or near the project folder, such as images/garden.jpg. File names and extensions must match exactly.\nLists\nUse <ol> for ordered steps and <ul> for items whose order is not important. Each item belongs inside an <li> element.\nTables\nUse <table> for tabular data, <tr> for a row, <th> for a header cell, and <td> for a data cell. Tables are for data, not general page layout.\nResponsible media use\nUse media you created or have permission to use. Add meaningful alt text and do not publish private photos or information without permission.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 4 – Activities\nHTML Multimedia, Lists, and Tables\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: Add Media to My Web Page\nInsert at least one relevant image and one audio or video file. Give the image meaningful alt text and add media controls. Test the page.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Multimedia Fix and Improve\nFix a missing image path, missing alt text, or media element without controls. Explain how you checked the result.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 3: Create My List Page\nCreate one ordered list of steps and one unordered list of items about a chosen topic.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 4: Fix and Organize the Lists\nCorrect incorrectly nested list tags and decide whether each list should be ordered or unordered.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 5: Build My First Table\nCreate a class schedule, price list, or contact list with a header row and at least three data rows.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 6: Fix and Improve the Table\nCorrect missing rows or cells and add meaningful table headings.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 4 – Quiz\nHTML Multimedia, Lists, and Tables\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. Which attribute identifies an image file?\nA. href\nB. src\nC. alt\nD. controls\n2. Why is alt text useful?\nA. It creates a table row\nB. It changes the browser\nC. It describes an image when it cannot be seen or loaded\nD. It plays audio\n3. Which element embeds video?\nA. <movie>\nB. <mediafile>\nC. <play>\nD. <video>\n4. Which attribute displays built-in media controls?\nA. controls\nB. src\nC. alt\nD. href\n5. Which list is suitable for numbered steps?\nA. <table>\nB. <ol>\nC. <ul>\nD. <li>\n6. Which element represents an item in a list?\nA. <td>\nB. <th>\nC. <li>\nD. <tr>\n7. Which element creates a table row?\nA. <row>\nB. <td>\nC. <th>\nD. <tr>\n8. Which element is commonly used for a table header cell?\nA. <th>\nB. <td>\nC. <li>\nD. <captiontext>\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."},"5":{"title":"HTML Forms and Links","competencies":"Utilize HTML form, types, and elements in a webpage.\nApply links to images, email, button, and text in a webpage.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – Introduction to HTML Forms and Form Elements","discussion":"Discuss the purpose of forms and common controls: input, label, radio button, checkbox, textarea, and button.","activities":"Show a sample form and explain the purpose of each control. Emphasize labels, appropriate input types, and safe use of sample data.","task":"Learners match form controls with their purposes and plan a simple school-appropriate sample form.","check":"Quick check: identify the correct control for a short scenario.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – Creating and Improving a Simple Form","discussion":"Demonstrate form structure and common input types, including text, radio, checkbox, and submit/button controls.","activities":"Build a sample form step by step. Explain that a static HTML form does not automatically store or send responses without a connected back-end service.","task":"Learners correct an incomplete form and label the controls. Discuss why practice forms should use fictional data.","check":"Collect a corrected form outline or code exercise.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – Applying Text, Image, Button, and Email Links","discussion":"Discuss anchor links and destinations, including links applied to text, images, buttons, and email addresses.","activities":"Demonstrate href, relative page links, mailto links, and a button that navigates to another page using a suitable method already taught. Test links in a browser.","task":"Learners identify broken links in a sample page and plan destinations for four link types.","check":"Exit ticket: distinguish a webpage link from an email link.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Activity: Create a Simple Form and Linked Web Page (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Create a demonstration form and a webpage containing appropriate links.","activities":"Demonstrate task requirements and explain safe sample data. Begin practical rotation. Provide local sample image assets if image links are required.","task":"Learners create the form and links. Waiting learners draw the form layout, identify input types, and write the destinations for text, image, button, and email links.","check":"Check form controls, labels, and link destinations. Confirm no sensitive data is used.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Activity: Fix and Enhance Forms and Links (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Troubleshoot form structure and correct broken or inappropriate links.","activities":"Model checking tag syntax, href values, file paths, and input types. Give practical time first to learners who have not yet completed individual work.","task":"Learners test each link and form control, correct errors, and demonstrate the result. Waiting learners complete a printed debugging exercise before their turn.","check":"Practical checklist and reflection: Which link did you test? What happens if the href destination is incorrect?\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 5 – Learner Notes\nHTML Forms and Links\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nPurpose of forms\nHTML forms collect information from users. They may contain text fields, radio buttons, checkboxes, selection controls, and buttons. Collect only information that is needed.\nForm element and labels\nThe <form> element groups controls. Use clear <label> elements; the label's for value should match the input's id.\nInput types\nCommon types include text, email, password, radio, and checkbox. Radio buttons suit one choice from a group; checkboxes suit multiple independent choices.\nButtons and processing\nA submit button submits a form. A button with type='button' does not submit by default. A real receiving service or script is needed to process form submissions; HTML alone does not securely store responses.\nHyperlinks\nThe <a> element creates a link and href specifies its destination. Links can be applied to text or images; a link can also be styled to look like a button.\nEmail links and safety\nA mailto: link can open an email application. Check destinations and never ask learners to submit passwords or sensitive personal data through a practice form.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 5 – Activities\nHTML Forms and Links\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: Create a Simple Online Form\nCreate a sample form with labeled text input, radio buttons, checkboxes, and a submit button. Use fictional data only.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Fix and Improve the Form\nCorrect mismatched labels and IDs, select suitable input types, and remove unnecessary or sensitive fields.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 3: Create a Linked Web Page\nCreate a text link, an image link, a button-style link, and an email link. Test every destination.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 4: Fix and Enhance Links\nCorrect missing or incorrect href values and test all text, image, button-style, and email links.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 5 – Quiz\nHTML Forms and Links\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. What is the main purpose of an HTML form?\nA. To store responses automatically without processing\nB. To collect information from users\nC. To style page backgrounds\nD. To create image files\n2. Which element groups form controls?\nA. <input-group-only>\nB. <submit>\nC. <form>\nD. <fieldset-page>\n3. Why should form inputs have clear labels?\nA. To make inputs invisible\nB. To replace the form element\nC. To automatically send the form\nD. So users understand what information is requested\n4. Which input type is suitable for one choice in a group?\nA. radio\nB. checkbox\nC. text\nD. file\n5. Which input type allows multiple independent selections?\nA. submit\nB. checkbox\nC. radio\nD. password\n6. Which element creates a hyperlink?\nA. <href>\nB. <url>\nC. <a>\nD. <linktext>\n7. Which attribute specifies a link destination?\nA. src\nB. alt\nC. controls\nD. href\n8. What does mailto: normally do?\nA. Opens an email application to compose a message\nB. Uploads an image\nC. Creates an HTML file\nD. Automatically sends a password\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."},"6":{"title":"Developing a Multi-page Website","competencies":"Develop a website.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – Understanding and Planning a Website","discussion":"Discuss the purpose, audience, content, and basic structure of a website. Review multi-page navigation.","activities":"Show a simple two-page website map. Explain home page, supporting page, navigation links, and project folder.","task":"Learners select a school-appropriate topic and draw a site map with at least two pages.","check":"Check that each learner has a clear topic, page titles, and planned navigation.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – Building the Home Page","discussion":"Review HTML structure, headings, paragraphs, lists, and images. Demonstrate building a home page.","activities":"Model creation of index.html with a heading, introduction, and organized content. Demonstrate saving in the project folder.","task":"Learners prepare page content and create or annotate a home-page structure. If devices are available, begin coding.","check":"Check page outline and basic HTML structure.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – Building the Second Page and Navigation","discussion":"Explain how to create a second HTML file and connect pages using relative links.","activities":"Demonstrate page2.html and links between index.html and page2.html. Test navigation in the browser.","task":"Learners create the second page plan and write navigation link code. Pair-check filenames and link paths.","check":"Quick practical or paper check: identify correct relative filenames and link destinations.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Activity: Plan and Build Your Website (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Create a two-page website with content and navigation.","activities":"Review the minimum output and demonstrate file organization and link testing. Start practical rotation. Learners waiting refine their site map and content plan.","task":"Learners create pages and navigation. Waiting learners prepare text, sketch layout, list required media, and write the HTML structure.","check":"Check at least two pages, relevant content, and navigation link structure.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Activity: Review and Improve Your Website (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Test and improve website structure, content, formatting, media, and navigation.","activities":"Model a website review: open each page, click every link, check media paths, and correct errors. Prioritize learners who still need individual practical time.","task":"Learners finish and demonstrate their website. Each learner personally tests page navigation. Waiting learners complete a website review checklist before their turn.","check":"Practical check: two pages open, navigation works both ways, content is organized, and files are in one folder.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 6 – Learner Notes\nDeveloping a Multi-page Website\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nPlan a website\nDecide the website's purpose, audience, pages, and content before coding. A simple website may contain Home and About pages.\nOrganize project files\nKeep pages and assets in one main project folder. Use clear file names and folders such as images. Make sure every file path matches the actual name and location.\nBuild consistent pages\nUse meaningful headings, paragraphs, images with alt text, lists, and links. Keep navigation and visual presentation consistent across pages.\nConnect pages\nUse relative links to connect pages in the project. For example, <a href='about.html'>About</a>. Provide a way to return to the Home page.\nReview and improve\nOpen every page in a browser. Test links, image paths, spelling, and navigation. Ask another person to test whether the site is easy to use.\nResponsible publishing\nUse permitted media, respect copyright, and avoid publishing private information. Review a site before sharing it publicly.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 6 – Activities\nDeveloping a Multi-page Website\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: Plan and Build Your Website\nPlan a website with at least two pages. Draw a simple sitemap, identify the audience, and create pages with headings, paragraphs, images, lists, and links.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Review and Improve Your Website\nTest navigation, fix broken links and image paths, improve formatting, and record at least three improvements.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 6 – Quiz\nDeveloping a Multi-page Website\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. What should be planned before building a website?\nA. Browser home page\nB. Purpose, audience, pages, and content\nC. Only background color\nD. Only image file size\n2. What is a sitemap?\nA. A CSS color palette\nB. A browser history file\nC. A plan showing website pages and their relationships\nD. A password list\n3. Which code links to another page in the same folder?\nA. <img src='about.html'>\nB. <p href='about.html'>About</p>\nC. <title>about.html</title>\nD. <a href='about.html'>About</a>\n4. Why organize project files?\nA. To locate and maintain pages and assets more easily\nB. To prevent users reading text\nC. To remove the need for links\nD. To make tags optional\n5. What should be tested before sharing a website?\nA. Only computer volume\nB. Navigation, links, images, spelling, and content\nC. Only the file icon\nD. Only desktop wallpaper\n6. What is a relative link?\nA. A computer setting\nB. A link without a destination\nC. A destination described relative to the current file or project\nD. A link that always needs a password\n7. Why add a Home link on inner pages?\nA. To submit a form\nB. To change file extension\nC. To hide the title\nD. To help visitors return to the main page\n8. Which is responsible publishing?\nA. Use permitted media and protect private information\nB. Copy any image without checking\nC. Publish private details without consent\nD. Include passwords on the page\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."},"7":{"title":"CSS Syntax, Selectors, Types, Colors, Backgrounds, Fonts, and Text","competencies":"Discuss syntax, selectors, and types of cascading style sheets.\nApply CSS colors, backgrounds, fonts, and text into a webpage.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – Introduction to CSS and CSS Syntax","discussion":"Discuss the purpose of CSS and the relationship between HTML structure and CSS presentation. Introduce selector, property, value, braces, and declarations.","activities":"Compare an unstyled webpage with a styled one. Demonstrate a basic CSS rule and identify its parts.","task":"Learners label selectors, properties, and values in sample rules and identify missing punctuation.","check":"Exit ticket: identify the three main parts of a CSS rule.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – Types of CSS","discussion":"Discuss inline, internal, and external CSS, including basic advantages and where each is written.","activities":"Demonstrate one simple style using each type. Emphasize the external stylesheet link and matching filename.","task":"Learners classify sample code as inline, internal, or external CSS and explain their choice.","check":"Short classification and file-link check.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – Selectors, Colors, Backgrounds, Fonts, and Text","discussion":"Discuss element and class selectors and selected styling properties for color, background, font, and text.","activities":"Demonstrate styling body, h1, p, and a class selector. Model readable contrast and consistent design.","task":"Learners create a paper style guide choosing a background, text color, heading style, and font for a sample webpage.","check":"Review style guide for readability and correct selector choices.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Activity: Style My Web Page (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Apply CSS rules to an HTML page using appropriate selectors and text styling.","activities":"Demonstrate linking style.css and applying a few basic rules. Begin individual practical rotations.","task":"Learners create or edit an external CSS file and style their webpage. Waiting learners write CSS rules on paper and identify where the stylesheet link belongs.","check":"Check stylesheet connection, selector use, and visible styling.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Activity: Fix and Organize CSS (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Correct CSS syntax errors and improve a page's colors, backgrounds, fonts, and text styles.","activities":"Model troubleshooting missing braces, semicolons, misspelled properties, and incorrect file paths. Prioritize learners needing computer time.","task":"Learners fix a provided stylesheet or refine their own page. Each learner demonstrates a CSS correction or style change.","check":"Practical checklist and reflection: Which CSS rule made the biggest visible difference?\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 7 – Learner Notes\nCSS Syntax, Selectors, Types, Colors, Backgrounds, Fonts, and Text\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nWhat is CSS?\nCSS means Cascading Style Sheets. It controls presentation such as colors, fonts, spacing, and layout. HTML provides structure; CSS controls appearance.\nCSS rule syntax\nA CSS rule has a selector and a declaration block. Example: p { color: navy; }. A declaration contains a property and value separated by a colon and usually ends with a semicolon.\nSelectors\nA type selector targets elements such as p. A class selector begins with a dot, such as .notice. An ID selector begins with #, such as #main-title. Classes can be reused; an ID should identify one unique element.\nTypes of CSS\nInline CSS is written in a style attribute. Internal CSS is written inside a <style> element. External CSS is stored in a separate .css file and connected with a <link> element.\nColors and backgrounds\nThe color property changes text color. background-color changes an element's background. Colors may use names, hexadecimal values, or RGB values.\nFonts and text\nfont-family selects a typeface, font-size controls size, font-weight controls weight, and text-align controls alignment. Choose readable fonts and sufficient contrast.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 7 – Activities\nCSS Syntax, Selectors, Types, Colors, Backgrounds, Fonts, and Text\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: Style My Web Page\nUse an HTML page and inline or external CSS to change text color, font, and background. Apply a type selector, class selector, and ID selector.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Fix and Organize CSS\nCorrect missing braces, colons, semicolons, and incorrect selectors. Group related rules and use meaningful class names.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 3: Colorful Web Makeover\nChoose coordinated background and text colors, font family, font size, and heading styles. Check readability and contrast.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 4: Style Doctor Challenge\nCorrect unreadable text, conflicting colors, and incorrect CSS rules. Explain how the changes improve readability.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 7 – Quiz\nCSS Syntax, Selectors, Types, Colors, Backgrounds, Fonts, and Text\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. What is the main role of CSS?\nA. To create internet connection\nB. To control the appearance of webpage content\nC. To define every HTML meaning\nD. To store form responses automatically\n2. Which is correct CSS rule structure?\nA. <selector: property=value>\nB. selector [value; property]\nC. selector { property: value; }\nD. property (selector) value\n3. Which selector targets paragraph elements?\nA. .p\nB. #p\nC. *p\nD. p\n4. Which selector targets a class named notice?\nA. .notice\nB. #notice\nC. notice()\nD. @notice\n5. Which selector targets the ID main-title?\nA. *main-title\nB. #main-title\nC. .main-title\nD. main-title\n6. Which CSS type is stored in a separate .css file?\nA. Internal CSS\nB. Browser CSS\nC. External CSS\nD. Inline CSS\n7. Which property changes background color?\nA. font-family\nB. text-align\nC. font-weight\nD. background-color\n8. Which property selects a typeface?\nA. font-family\nB. background-color\nC. border-style\nD. text-decoration\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."},"8":{"title":"CSS Borders, Margins, Padding, Icons, Links, Lists, Tables, Animation, and Transition","competencies":"Apply CSS borders, margins, and paddings.\nApply CSS icons, links, list and tables.\nApply CSS animation and transition in a webpage.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – CSS Borders, Margins, and Padding","discussion":"Discuss border, margin, and padding and how they affect the layout of webpage elements.","activities":"Use a simple box example to demonstrate border, outside spacing, and inside spacing. Compare the result of changing each property.","task":"Learners label margin, border, and padding in a diagram and suggest values for a sample content box.","check":"Check learners can distinguish the three spacing properties.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – Styling Links, Lists, Tables, and Icons","discussion":"Discuss how CSS can improve links, lists, tables, and icons. Explain hover states and readable contrast.","activities":"Demonstrate link color and hover styling, list spacing, table borders, and basic icon use if an approved resource is available.","task":"Learners mark design improvements on a sample page and write CSS rules for a link, list, or table.","check":"Review sample CSS and design decisions.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – CSS Animation and Transition","discussion":"Explain the difference between transition and animation at an introductory level. Discuss simple, non-distracting effects.","activities":"Demonstrate a transition on hover and a simple keyframe animation if within available lesson coverage. Avoid flashing or excessive movement.","task":"Learners predict how a button or element will change and identify the relevant CSS property or keyframe steps.","check":"Quick check: distinguish a hover transition from a defined animation.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Activity: Improve Page Layout and Visual Effects (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Apply borders, spacing, styles for links/lists/tables, and a simple transition or animation as taught.","activities":"Demonstrate the expected changes and remind learners to prioritize readability. Start practical rotation.","task":"Learners improve their existing webpage. Waiting learners sketch the before-and-after layout and prepare CSS rules on paper.","check":"Check border, margin, padding, styled list/table or link, and transition/animation if taught.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Activity: Style Doctor Challenge (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Correct layout and styling problems and demonstrate improved visual presentation.","activities":"Model checking CSS syntax and adjusting spacing and effects. Prioritize learners who still need individual computer practice.","task":"Learners repair a teacher-provided CSS sample or refine their own work. Each learner demonstrates at least one working style correction.","check":"Practical checklist and reflection: How did spacing improve the page? Which effect should be used sparingly?\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 8 – Learner Notes\nCSS Borders, Margins, Padding, Icons, Links, Lists, Tables, Animations, and Transitions\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nBorders\nThe border property draws a line around an element. It can set width, style, and color, for example border: 1px solid gray;.\nMargin and padding\nMargin is space outside the border. Padding is space between content and border. Both help create a clear and comfortable layout.\nStyling links\nCSS can style link colors and appearance. The :hover pseudo-class applies styles when a pointer is over an element. Keep links identifiable and provide visible keyboard focus.\nLists and tables\nCSS can adjust list spacing, table borders, cell padding, fonts, and colors. Keep headings clear and content readable.\nIcons\nIcons can support navigation and communicate actions. Use trusted or permitted icon files and provide text labels or accessible names when needed.\nTransitions\nA transition gradually changes a CSS property when its value changes. It is often used for hover or focus feedback.\nAnimations\nCSS animations can change styles over time using @keyframes and the animation property. Use movement sparingly so it does not distract.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 8 – Activities\nCSS Borders, Margins, Padding, Icons, Links, Lists, Tables, Animations, and Transitions\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: Box It Up!\nAdd borders to selected sections and adjust margins and padding to create clear separation.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Spacing Master Challenge\nCorrect uneven spacing by fixing border, margin, and padding rules. Explain the difference between margin and padding.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 3: Link & Icon Adventure\nAdd useful icons and links. Style link colors, sizes, and hover effects. Ensure each link has a clear purpose and accessible text.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 4: Stylish Lists & Tables\nStyle ordered and unordered lists and a data table using borders, spacing, colors, and readable fonts.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 5: Animate Your Page!\nUse @keyframes to animate one suitable element. Keep the effect simple and non-distracting.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 6: Smooth Transition Challenge\nApply a transition to a button, link, or image. Test hover and keyboard focus behavior.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 8 – Quiz\nCSS Borders, Margins, Padding, Icons, Links, Lists, Tables, Animations, and Transitions\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. What does border create?\nA. A hyperlink\nB. A line around an element\nC. Space outside an element\nD. Space between content and border\n2. Which property controls space outside the border?\nA. font-size\nB. opacity\nC. margin\nD. padding\n3. Which property controls space between content and border?\nA. margin\nB. display\nC. text-align\nD. padding\n4. Which pseudo-class styles an element on pointer hover?\nA. :hover\nB. ::before\nC. :visited\nD. @media\n5. Which CSS feature defines animation steps?\nA. @transition\nB. @keyframes\nC. @selectors\nD. @frameset\n6. What does a CSS transition do?\nA. Stores form responses\nB. Adds a table row\nC. Gradually changes a property when its value changes\nD. Creates an HTML page\n7. Why should icons have accessible labels when needed?\nA. To animate automatically\nB. To remove link destinations\nC. To prevent text selection\nD. So users can understand their meaning or action\n8. What is a good animation practice?\nA. Use simple effects that do not distract\nB. Animate every element continuously\nC. Make text flash rapidly\nD. Hide important content\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."},"9":{"title":"CSS Box Model, Box Shadow, and Transform","competencies":"Utilize CSS box model in a webpage.\nUtilize CSS box shadow and transform in a webpage.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – Understanding the CSS Box Model","discussion":"Discuss content, padding, border, and margin and their positions around an element.","activities":"Draw and explain the box model. Demonstrate how changing padding, border, and margin affects a content box.","task":"Learners label the four parts of a box model diagram and answer a short scenario exercise.","check":"Check correct identification and order of the box model parts.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – Applying Box Model Properties","discussion":"Demonstrate width, margin, padding, border, and box-sizing where included in the lesson.","activities":"Show how to adjust a content card and observe the page. Discuss spacing and alignment.","task":"Learners calculate or compare sample box dimensions conceptually and write a CSS rule for a card.","check":"Review CSS rule accuracy and spacing choices.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – Box Shadow and Transform","discussion":"Discuss the purpose of box-shadow and transform. Introduce simple scale, rotate, and translate examples.","activities":"Demonstrate subtle shadow and transform effects. Explain that effects should support rather than distract from content.","task":"Learners choose a suitable element for a shadow or transform and sketch the expected result.","check":"Ask learners to identify the effect produced by a sample transform.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Activity: CSS Box Model Makeover (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Apply box model properties to webpage sections or cards and add a subtle shadow or transform.","activities":"Demonstrate the practical requirements and how to inspect changes in the browser. Start individual rotations.","task":"Learners style at least two content boxes. Waiting learners draw and label the box model and write proposed CSS properties and values.","check":"Check spacing, border, padding, margin, and box dimensions.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Activity: Perfectly Padded Page / Shadow Play / Transform It (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Refine spacing and demonstrate box-shadow and transform properties.","activities":"Model how to check for overlap and test a small transform. Prioritize learners who still need computer time.","task":"Learners complete and demonstrate their improved page. Each learner shows at least one box-shadow or transform effect and explains it.","check":"Practical checklist and reflection: Which box model property did you adjust and why?\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 9 – Learner Notes\nCSS Box Model, Box Shadow, and Transform\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nThe CSS box model\nEach element is treated as a rectangular box made of content, padding, border, and margin. Content is the text or media; padding surrounds content; border surrounds padding; margin creates outside space.\nDimensions\nWith the default content-box model, width and height describe the content box. Padding and border add to the total size. box-sizing: border-box includes padding and border within the declared width and height.\nBox shadow\nThe box-shadow property adds a shadow around an element. Use subtle shadows so text and controls remain clear.\nTransform\nThe transform property changes visual position, size, or rotation. Common functions include translate(), scale(), and rotate(). A transform does not change the normal document flow.\nTest and refine\nCheck whether boxes overlap, content is clipped, or spacing becomes inconsistent. Use transformations carefully and preserve readability.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 9 – Activities\nCSS Box Model, Box Shadow, and Transform\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: Box Model Makeover\nCreate a section or card and adjust content width, padding, border, and margin. Draw and label the four parts of the box model.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Perfectly Padded Page\nInspect uneven spacing and adjust margin, padding, and width to improve alignment and readability.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 3: Shadow Play\nApply a subtle box-shadow to an image, button, or div. Compare the original and improved versions.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 4: Transform It\nApply scale(), rotate(), or translate() to selected elements. Record the visual change and check that the layout remains usable.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 9 – Quiz\nCSS Box Model, Box Shadow, and Transform\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. Which four parts make up the CSS box model?\nA. Header, body, footer, and nav\nB. Content, padding, border, and margin\nC. Content, font, color, and link\nD. Width, height, image, and text\n2. Which part is directly around the content?\nA. Border\nB. Outline\nC. Padding\nD. Margin\n3. Which part is outside the border?\nA. Content\nB. Padding\nC. Font\nD. Margin\n4. What does box-sizing: border-box do?\nA. Includes padding and border within declared width and height\nB. Removes all padding\nC. Turns a div into a table\nD. Adds a shadow\n5. Which property adds a shadow around an element?\nA. border-collapse\nB. box-shadow\nC. text-align\nD. font-style\n6. Which transform function changes size?\nA. translate()\nB. skew-only()\nC. scale()\nD. rotate()\n7. Which transform function moves an element visually?\nA. scale()\nB. font-family()\nC. border()\nD. translate()\n8. What should be checked after transforms?\nA. Overlapping, clipping, and readability\nB. Only file name\nC. Computer volume\nD. Download folder\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."},"10":{"title":"Creating an Interactive Website","competencies":"Create an interactive website.","days":[{"day":1,"title":"Day 1 – Understanding Website Interactivity","discussion":"Discuss what makes a website interactive, using links, buttons, forms, and multimedia as examples from the BOW.","activities":"Demonstrate a page with a clickable button, form control, and navigation link. Ask learners to identify the user action and visible response.","task":"Learners classify examples as navigation or interaction and plan one useful interaction for a sample webpage.","check":"Exit ticket: describe the user action and expected response.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":2,"title":"Day 2 – Planning My Interactive World","discussion":"Guide learners to choose a school-appropriate topic and plan the page purpose, content, and user interaction.","activities":"Model a simple page plan: heading, instructions, control, expected response, and navigation. Review safe and responsible content choices.","task":"Learners draw a wireframe and write a short interaction plan: When the user does ____, the webpage should ____.","check":"Review each plan for a clear action and visible response.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":3,"title":"Day 3 – Guided Practice: Build and Test One Interaction","discussion":"Demonstrate a basic interactive element using the techniques included in the BOW and lessons already taught.","activities":"Model adding a button or form control and producing a visible response. If JavaScript is not yet part of the approved lesson coverage, use HTML/CSS interactions such as links and hover effects instead.","task":"Learners follow the guided example, identify the control, action, and response, and troubleshoot a sample.","check":"Check that learners can explain how the interaction works.","practicalGuide":""},{"day":4,"title":"Day 4 – Hands-on Activity: My Interactive World (Practical Rotation A)","discussion":"Develop a webpage with working links, buttons, forms, or multimedia interactions as taught.","activities":"Explain the practical output and demonstrate the testing process. Start individual practical rotations. Waiting learners complete the wireframe, content plan, and interaction flow on paper.","task":"Learners build their interactive webpage. Waiting learners prepare text, identify controls, and write expected actions and responses before their turn.","check":"Check at least one working interaction, clear instructions, and readable page layout.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."},{"day":5,"title":"Day 5 – Hands-on Activity: Interactive Website Makeover (Practical Rotation B / Completion)","discussion":"Improve the functionality, usability, and presentation of the interactive webpage.","activities":"Model testing every control, correcting links, and improving labels and instructions. Prioritize learners who still need individual practical time.","task":"Learners finish and demonstrate the website. Each learner personally tests the interaction and explains the visible response. Waiting learners use the test checklist before their turn.","check":"Practical demonstration and reflection. Record completion and identify learners needing follow-up before the suggested performance task.\nSuggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task.","practicalGuide":"Suggested 45-minute practical-period flow:\n5 minutes – attendance, task briefing, and safety/file reminders.\n5 minutes – teacher demonstration of the target steps and output.\n25 minutes – practical rotation: learners work individually in available computer slots; learners waiting complete the paper preparation/debugging task.\n5 minutes – save work, teacher checks progress, and learners explain one step.\n5 minutes – exit reflection and record of learners who need a completion turn.\nRotation note: Divide the class according to the actual number of working computers. With approximately 50 learners and 35 functional laptops, learners who do not receive a full individual turn on Day 4 should receive priority computer time on Day 5. Learners who finish early improve their output or peer-review; they should not take another learner's reserved practical turn.\nPaper-based preparation while waiting: learners sketch the expected webpage, plan content and filenames, arrange or annotate code, identify tags/properties, or troubleshoot a printed sample. This is preparatory work and does not replace the individual computer task."}],"resources":{"notes":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 10 – Learner Notes\nCreating an Interactive Website\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nKey Concepts\nInteractive websites\nAn interactive website responds to user actions. Examples include clicking navigation links, activating buttons, submitting forms, selecting options, or controlling media.\nHTML structure and controls\nHTML links, buttons, forms, and input controls provide the parts users interact with. Links navigate; buttons perform actions.\nCSS feedback\nCSS can show hover and focus states, highlight controls, and provide visual feedback. Interactive elements should be easy to identify and operate.\nForms and responses\nForms allow users to enter information. A working form needs appropriate validation and a receiving process. A static HTML form alone does not securely save or transmit information.\nMultimedia interaction\nImages and media can be linked or controlled. Explain what will happen when a user selects an item. Provide alternative text and accessible controls.\nUsability and safety\nTest with mouse and keyboard. Use clear labels, readable text, consistent navigation, and visible focus. Do not collect sensitive information unnecessarily.\nRemember\nUse correct and meaningful HTML/CSS elements and properties.\nKeep code organized and readable.\nSave files with correct names and extensions.\nTest your work in a browser and correct errors.\nRespect copyright, privacy, and accessibility.\nQuick Review\nWrite three important terms and define each.\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat is one common mistake to avoid?\n________________________________________________________________________\nWhat will you check before submitting your work?\n________________________________________________________________________","activities":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 10 – Activities\nCreating an Interactive Website\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nDirections: Complete the activities as assigned. If a computer is unavailable, complete planning, code-reading, and correction tasks on paper.\nActivity 1: My Interactive World\nCreate a webpage with at least one navigation link, a button, a form, and an interactive image or multimedia element. Each element must have a clear purpose and visible response.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nActivity 2: Interactive Website Makeover\nImprove an existing webpage by adding or refining hover effects, clickable images, forms, or styled buttons. Test navigation and usability.\nOutput / Answer:\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nReflection: What did you learn or improve?\n________________________________________________________________________\nSubmission Checklist\nI followed the instructions.\nMy output is complete.\nI checked my work for errors.\nI used appropriate and permitted content.\nI can explain the steps I performed.","quiz":"TLE–ICT LEARNING HUB\nWeek 10 – Quiz\nCreating an Interactive Website\nGrade 9 and Grade 10 | TLE–ICT Computer Programming | SY 2026–2027\nName: __________________________ Grade & Section: __________________________\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the best answer.\n1. What makes a website interactive?\nA. It has no controls\nB. It responds to user actions\nC. It contains only large images\nD. It uses one background color\n2. Which element is intended for an action on the current page?\nA. <meta>\nB. <head>\nC. <button>\nD. <title>\n3. Which element is primarily used to navigate to another page?\nA. <button> only\nB. <p>\nC. <strong>\nD. <a>\n4. What does a visible focus style help users do?\nA. Identify the interactive element selected by keyboard navigation\nB. Download the webpage\nC. Rename the HTML file\nD. Hide form labels\n5. Why should form labels be clear?\nA. To hide field purpose\nB. To explain what information each control requests\nC. To submit without processing\nD. To replace input controls\n6. Does a static HTML form automatically store responses?\nA. Yes, HTML sends it to the teacher\nB. No, forms cannot collect information\nC. No; it needs an appropriate receiving process or service\nD. Yes, every form saves automatically\n7. Which is an example of interaction?\nA. A heading appears without user action\nB. A file is renamed in File Explorer\nC. A wallpaper changes\nD. A button responds when a user activates it\n8. What should be tested before presenting an interactive website?\nA. Links, buttons, forms, keyboard use, and visible responses\nB. Only wallpaper\nC. Only title color\nD. Only browser history\nScore: ______ / 8"},"assessmentScale":"Suggested practical scale: 4 – complete and works independently; 3 – mostly complete with minor errors; 2 – partly complete and needs support; 1 – not yet demonstrated. Adapt scoring to the approved assessment plan."}};
